-- 1. [Chemistry of the Groups · Skill 2 · ans C] id 281971ff-47d5-48f2-ade7-bb49b09ecf78
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A strip of an unlabeled, freshly exposed metal is lowered into cold water in a fume hood. It reacts at once, steadily evolving a flammable gas, and the water that drains off turns red litmus blue. A \(0.460\ \text{g}\) strip is consumed completely, and the gas is collected over the run at \(298\ \text{K}\) and \(1.00\ \text{atm}\), occupying \(0.245\ \text{L}\). The metal's ionic charge is not yet known. Using \(R = 0.0821\ \text{L·atm·mol}^{-1}\text{·K}^{-1}\), the element most likely belongs to which group of the periodic table?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"Group 2, the alkaline earth metals, because assigning a \\(+2\\) cation gives a \\(1{:}1\\) metal-to-\\(\\text{H}_2\\) ratio and a molar mass near \\(46\\ \\text{g/mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"Group 13, the boron group, because assigning a \\(+3\\) cation gives a \\(2{:}3\\) metal-to-\\(\\text{H}_2\\) ratio and a molar mass near \\(69\\ \\text{g/mol}\\), matching gallium"},{"label":"C","text":"Group 1, the alkali metals, because assigning a \\(+1\\) cation gives a \\(2{:}1\\) metal-to-\\(\\text{H}_2\\) ratio and a molar mass near \\(23\\ \\text{g/mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Group 17, the halogens, because a reactive nonmetal of this kind displaces \\(\\text{H}_2\\) from water and leaves an acidic solution"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is C because the observations fix the species as an active, hydroxide-forming metal but leave its charge open, so the group can be settled only by testing each charge against the gas data and the cold-water constraint together. First reduce the gas data: \(n_{\text{H}_2} = \dfrac{PV}{RT} = \dfrac{(1.00\ \text{atm})(0.245\ \text{L})}{(0.0821)(298\ \text{K})} = 0.0100\ \text{mol}\). An active metal that liberates \(\text{H}_2\) from cold water and leaves a basic solution follows \(2\,\text{M} + 2z\,\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\,\text{M(OH)}_z + z\,\text{H}_2\), so a cation charge \(z\) sets a metal-to-\(\text{H}_2\) ratio of \(2{:}z\); because both alkali metals and the heavier alkaline earth metals react with cold water to give basic solutions, the charge cannot be read off the qualitative clues and each value of \(z\) must be tested. For \(z = +1\) the ratio is \(2{:}1\), so \(n_{\text{M}} = 2(0.0100) = 0.0200\ \text{mol}\) and the molar mass is \(\dfrac{0.460\ \text{g}}{0.0200\ \text{mol}} = 23.0\ \text{g/mol}\), which matches sodium \((22.99\ \text{g/mol})\), a Group 1 metal that reacts with cold water to liberate \(\text{H}_2\) and generate a strong base. This is the only charge that yields a molar mass belonging to a real metal whose cold-water behavior matches the observations, so the element is in Group 1. (Choice A) Assigning \(+2\) gives the \(1{:}1\) ratio \(n_{\text{M}} = n_{\text{H}_2} = 0.0100\ \text{mol}\) and a molar mass of \(46\ \text{g/mol}\), but no alkaline earth metal has that mass: calcium is \(40.08\ \text{g/mol}\) and strontium is \(87.62\ \text{g/mol}\), and the nearest element, scandium \((44.96\ \text{g/mol})\), lies in Group 3, so the \(+2\) assignment names no Group 2 element. (Choice B) Assigning \(+3\) gives the \(2{:}3\) ratio \(n_{\text{M}} = \tfrac{2}{3}(0.0100) = 0.00667\ \text{mol}\) and a molar mass near \(69\ \text{g/mol}\), numerically close to gallium, but gallium is not vigorously reactive toward cold water and does not steadily evolve \(\text{H}_2\) while producing a strongly basic solution, so this charge satisfies the arithmetic yet contradicts the cold-water observation. (Choice D) The halogens are nonmetals that do not displace \(\text{H}_2\) from water; their aqueous hydrides, such as \(\text{HCl}\), give acidic solutions that turn litmus red rather than blue, reversing both the metal-versus-nonmetal character and the base-versus-acid outcome. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must convert gas-law data into moles, propagate each candidate cation charge through a stoichiometric ratio to a distinct molar mass, and combine that result with the cold-water reactivity constraint to identify the one charge that names a real element consistent with every observation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '281971ff-47d5-48f2-ade7-bb49b09ecf78';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '281971ff-47d5-48f2-ade7-bb49b09ecf78';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('281971ff-47d5-48f2-ade7-bb49b09ecf78', 'A', 'partial_truth', $a$Assumes a +2 cation (1:1 ratio) because the cold-water/basic-solution clues also fit the heavier alkaline earth metals, computes a molar mass of 46 g/mol, but skips the check that no Group 2 element has that mass (Ca=40, Sr=88; 46 falls on Group 3 scandium)$a$),
  ('281971ff-47d5-48f2-ade7-bb49b09ecf78', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Assumes a +3 cation (2:3 ratio), reaches molar mass ~69 g/mol matching gallium, and accepts the numerical near-match while ignoring that gallium does not react with cold water to evolve H2 and form a strong base$a$),
  ('281971ff-47d5-48f2-ade7-bb49b09ecf78', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the metal/nonmetal and base/acid relationships, treating the active metal as a halogen nonmetal that yields an acidic solution$a$);

-- 2. [Chemistry of the Groups · Skill 2 · ans B] id 73b5fcb7-a512-42ca-8ec3-b430c9264f47
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A metal whose neutral atoms have the ground-state configuration \([\text{Ar}]4s^2\) is heated with the diatomic gas of a Group 17 element whose neutral atoms have the valence configuration \(3s^2 3p^5\), and the metal is consumed completely. When \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of the metal reacts, exactly \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of the halogen gas is taken up. Assuming each element reaches the charge favored by its group's electronic structure, which formula is most consistent with both the group-dictated charges and the measured mole ratio?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{MX}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{MX}_2\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{MX}_4\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{M}_2\\text{X}_3\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is B because the formula must satisfy two independent requirements at once. First, the group-dictated charges: a neutral \([\text{Ar}]4s^2\) atom is a Group 2 (alkaline-earth) metal that loses both \(ns^2\) electrons to reach the argon core, giving \(\text{M}^{2+}\); a Group 17 atom with a \(3s^2 3p^5\) valence shell gains one electron to complete its octet, giving \(\text{X}^-\). Charge balance therefore predicts two \(-1\) anions per \(+2\) cation, i.e. \(\text{MX}_2\). Second, the mole data must agree: the halogen is supplied as the diatomic molecule \(\text{X}_2\), so \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of gas contains \(0.0500 \times 2 = 0.100\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{X}\) atoms. Dividing by the \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of metal gives a ratio of \(0.100 / 0.0500 = 2\) halogen atoms per metal atom, which is exactly \(\text{MX}_2\). Both the electronic-structure argument and the stoichiometric measurement converge on \(\text{MX}_2\). (Choice A) \(\text{MX}\) results from treating the \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of halogen gas as \(0.0500\ \text{mol}\) of \(\text{X}\) atoms, that is, from neglecting that the halogen is diatomic; this gives a false 1:1 ratio and also conflicts with the \(\text{M}^{2+}/\text{X}^-\) charges, which cannot balance in a 1:1 unit. (Choice C) \(\text{MX}_4\) arises from applying the diatomic factor of two a second time (e.g. multiplying the \(0.100\ \text{mol}\) of atoms by another factor of two), yielding a spurious 4:1 ratio; no Group 2/Group 17 charge combination produces a \(+4/-1\) pairing. (Choice D) \(\text{M}_2\text{X}_3\) corresponds to a \(+3/-2\) crisscross, which requires the metal to be \(\text{M}^{3+}\) and the halogen to be \(\text{X}^{2-}\); a Group 2 atom does not surrender a third electron from its noble-gas core and a Group 17 atom needs only one electron to complete its octet, so neither charge is supported, and the 2:3 ratio also contradicts the measured 2:1 atom ratio. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the group-based charge assignments with a diatomic-to-atom mole conversion and a ratio calculation, then check that the chemical and quantitative results agree before selecting the formula.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '73b5fcb7-a512-42ca-8ec3-b430c9264f47';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '73b5fcb7-a512-42ca-8ec3-b430c9264f47';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('73b5fcb7-a512-42ca-8ec3-b430c9264f47', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Treats moles of diatomic X2 gas as moles of X atoms, dropping the factor of 2 and getting a false 1:1 ratio$a$),
  ('73b5fcb7-a512-42ca-8ec3-b430c9264f47', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Applies the diatomic-to-atom factor of 2 twice, inflating the halogen-atom count to a spurious 4:1 ratio$a$),
  ('73b5fcb7-a512-42ca-8ec3-b430c9264f47', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Crisscrosses incorrect charges (M3+ and X2-) instead of the group-dictated +2 and -1, yielding a 2:3 formula$a$);

-- 3. [Chemistry of the Groups · Skill 2 · ans D] id b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist measures the first three successive ionization energies (in \(\text{kJ/mol}\)) of two freshly cut period-3 metals, \(\text{X}\) and \(\text{Y}\), exposed to moist air: for \(\text{X}\), \(\text{IE}_1 = 496\), \(\text{IE}_2 = 4562\), \(\text{IE}_3 = 6912\); for \(\text{Y}\), \(\text{IE}_1 = 738\), \(\text{IE}_2 = 1451\), \(\text{IE}_3 = 7733\). Each metal is allowed to react only as far as its stable noble-gas-core cation. Which statement most likely identifies the metal that tarnishes faster and the total energy needed to reach that faster metal's stable cation?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{X}\\) tarnishes faster, and reaching its stable \\(\\text{X}^{2+}\\) cation requires \\(738\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{X}\\) tarnishes faster, and reaching its stable \\(\\text{X}^{2+}\\) cation requires \\(5058\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Y}\\) tarnishes faster, and reaching its stable \\(\\text{Y}^{2+}\\) cation requires \\(2189\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{X}\\) tarnishes faster, and reaching its stable \\(\\text{X}^+\\) cation requires \\(496\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is D because three pieces of evidence must be chained. First, reactivity (rate of tarnishing) is governed by how easily the FIRST valence electron leaves, i.e. by \(\text{IE}_1\): \(\text{X}\) has \(\text{IE}_1 = 496\ \text{kJ/mol}\) versus \(738\ \text{kJ/mol}\) for \(\text{Y}\), so \(\text{X}\) loses its first electron more readily and tarnishes faster — \(\text{X}\) is the Group 1 alkali metal (\([\text{Ne}]3s^1\), sodium) and \(\text{Y}\) is the Group 2 alkaline earth metal (\([\text{Ne}]3s^2\), magnesium). Second, the stable cation is set by the noble-gas core, which is flagged by the large jump in successive ionization energy: for \(\text{X}\), the jump is between \(\text{IE}_1\) (496) and \(\text{IE}_2\) (4562, a roughly ninefold rise), showing only one electron lies outside the \([\text{Ne}]\) core, so \(\text{X}\) stops at \(\text{X}^+\). Third, the energy to reach that stable cation is therefore just \(\text{IE}_1 = 496\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Thus the more reactive metal forms the lower-charge ion, and only \(\text{IE}_1\) is summed. (Choice A) correctly identifies \(\text{X}\) as faster-tarnishing but mismatches the charge and the energy: it assigns \(\text{X}\) a \(2+\) charge yet uses \(738\ \text{kJ/mol}\), which is \(\text{Y}\)'s \(\text{IE}_1\), so it both overstates \(\text{X}\)'s stable charge past its \([\text{Ne}]\) core and pulls the energy value from the wrong element. (Choice B) keeps the correct, more-reactive metal \(\text{X}\) but incorrectly ties ease of first ionization to a higher charge, adding \(\text{IE}_1 + \text{IE}_2 = 496 + 4562 = 5058\ \text{kJ/mol}\) to reach \(\text{X}^{2+}\); removing that second electron breaks into the filled \([\text{Ne}]\) core, which the ninefold ionization jump shows is prohibitively costly, so \(\text{X}\) does not form \(\text{X}^{2+}\) under these conditions. (Choice C) reverses the reactivity relationship by assuming the metal able to release more electrons, \(\text{Y}\), is the more reactive one; \(\text{Y}\)'s higher \(\text{IE}_1\) of 738 makes it tarnish more slowly, and although \(2189\ \text{kJ/mol} = 738 + 1451\) is the correct energy for \(\text{Y}\) to reach \(\text{Y}^{2+}\), \(\text{Y}\) is not the faster-tarnishing metal the stem asks about. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the periodic ionization-energy trend, read the successive-ionization jump to locate each metal's noble-gas core, and sum only the ionization steps that precede that core to compute the energy to the correct stable cation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Identifies the faster metal correctly but assigns it a 2+ charge and grabs Y's IE1 (738) as the energy, pulling the numeric value from the wrong element and ignoring the ionization-jump diagnostic for the core.$a$),
  ('b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Ties ease of first ionization to a higher ionic charge (assumes the more reactive metal loses more electrons), summing IE1+IE2 = 5058 kJ/mol to reach X-2+ instead of stopping at the noble-gas core.$a$),
  ('b7858478-c39f-4aeb-bb6b-32894e259ab7', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses reactivity by assuming the metal that can release two electrons (Y) is the more reactive one; correctly computes 738+1451 = 2189 kJ/mol for Y-2+ but reports the wrong (slower-tarnishing) metal.$a$);

-- 4. [Chemistry of the Groups · Skill 2 · ans A] id eee33c57-0002-4666-81a6-f7a1fb97a1b9
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$During a demonstration, a technician drops freshly cut cubes of magnesium, calcium, strontium, and barium (all group 2) into separate beakers of cold liquid water and times how long each sample sustains a steady stream of \( \text{H}_2 \) bubbles. The technician recalls two periodic facts: the ease of stripping both \( ns^2 \) electrons to form \( \text{M}^{2+} \) increases down the group, and the aqueous solubility of the group-2 hydroxides also increases down the group, so that \( \text{Mg(OH)}_2 \) is nearly insoluble while \( \text{Ba(OH)}_2 \) is appreciably soluble. Given that the hydroxide produced can deposit on the metal surface, which sample will most likely sustain the fastest observable rate of gas evolution?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"Barium, because it both loses its two valence electrons most readily and forms the most soluble hydroxide, so its surface stays exposed and the reaction is not throttled."},{"label":"B","text":"Magnesium, because its small, high-charge-density cation makes its valence electrons the easiest in the set to remove and drives the most vigorous reaction."},{"label":"C","text":"Magnesium, because it forms the least soluble hydroxide, which precipitates fastest and keeps the reaction surface continually refreshed."},{"label":"D","text":"Calcium, because it sits midway in the group and therefore balances a strong driving force against minimal surface coating better than either extreme."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is A because the observable rate is set by TWO group-2 trends acting together, and only the lowest member of the set is favored by both. First, the reaction with water requires each metal atom to surrender its two valence \( ns^2 \) electrons to form \( \text{M}^{2+} \); descending group 2, those electrons occupy higher principal levels and are more shielded, so the combined energy to remove them falls and the thermodynamic driving force for reaction grows. By that trend alone barium, the lowest member, is the most strongly driven. Second, the rate that is actually observed also depends on whether the hydroxide product armors the surface: the group-2 hydroxides become more soluble down the group, so \( \text{Mg(OH)}_2 \) is nearly insoluble and forms an adherent passivating coat that chokes the reaction, whereas the appreciably soluble \( \text{Ba(OH)}_2 \) dissolves away and keeps fresh barium metal in contact with the water. Both the driving-force trend and the surface-exposure trend point to barium, so barium sustains the fastest observable gas evolution. (Choice B) The premise that the smallest, highest-charge-density cation loses its valence electrons most easily reverses the real ionization trend; the compact \( \text{Mg}^{2+} \) arises precisely because magnesium's valence electrons are the most tightly held in the set, making its reaction the slowest, not the most vigorous. (Choice C) This choice correctly identifies \( \text{Mg(OH)}_2 \) as the least soluble hydroxide but inverts its kinetic consequence: an insoluble hydroxide deposits as an adherent passivating film that shields the surface and suppresses further reaction, rather than refreshing it. (Choice D) Selecting the middle element treats the two trends as opposing forces needing a compromise, but here both the ionization-ease trend and the hydroxide-solubility trend run the same direction down the group, so the lowest member is optimal on both counts and no balancing is required. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a thermodynamic periodic trend with a solubility-driven kinetic trend and recognize that both select the same element rather than treating either factor in isolation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'eee33c57-0002-4666-81a6-f7a1fb97a1b9';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'eee33c57-0002-4666-81a6-f7a1fb97a1b9';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('eee33c57-0002-4666-81a6-f7a1fb97a1b9', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Applies a reversed ionization/size relationship, assuming the smallest high-charge-density cation means the valence electrons are easiest to remove, when in fact Mg holds its electrons most tightly.$a$),
  ('eee33c57-0002-4666-81a6-f7a1fb97a1b9', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Correctly identifies Mg(OH)2 as least soluble but reverses the kinetic effect of a passivating coat, treating an adherent insoluble film as surface-refreshing rather than surface-blocking.$a$),
  ('eee33c57-0002-4666-81a6-f7a1fb97a1b9', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$Treats the two periodic trends as opposing and picks a 'balanced' middle element, missing that both trends run the same direction down the group so the extreme (Ba) wins on both.$a$);

-- 5. [Periodic Table Structure & Classification · Skill 2 · ans B] id 3a0f7eef-704a-4722-91bd-88c874f3a554
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A main-group metal forms a stable \(3+\) cation whose ground-state electron configuration is \([\text{Kr}]4d^{10}\). Working only from this ion configuration and the aufbau filling order, where on a periodic table (period number down the left edge, group number across the top) should the neutral parent element most likely be located?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"Period 5, Group 10"},{"label":"B","text":"Period 5, Group 13"},{"label":"C","text":"Period 5, Group 12"},{"label":"D","text":"Period 5, Group 7"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is B because the configuration given describes the \(3+\) cation, not the neutral atom, so the neutral element must first be reconstructed. A \(3+\) charge means the atom lost three electrons, so the neutral atom has three MORE electrons than the ion: starting from \([\text{Kr}]4d^{10}\), the three returned electrons fill the next available subshells in aufbau order. The \(4d\) subshell is already complete (it holds a maximum of ten), so the electrons enter \(5s\) (two electrons) and then \(5p\) (one electron), giving the neutral configuration \([\text{Kr}]4d^{10}5s^2 5p^1\). The period equals the largest principal quantum number in this neutral configuration, which is \(5\) (from \(5s\) and \(5p\)), placing the element in Period 5. The group of a p-block element follows from its valence shell: an outer \(5s^2 5p^1\) arrangement is the \(ns^2 np^1\) valence pattern of Group 13, with the filled \(4d^{10}\) lying one shell below the valence shell and not entering the group count. Period 5 and Group 13 identify the element as indium (In, \(Z = 49\)). (Choice A) Period 5, Group 10 results from reading the supplied \([\text{Kr}]4d^{10}\) as the neutral atom and ignoring the \(3+\) charge; that configuration is in fact the ground state of palladium (\(Z = 46\)), a d-block element in Group 10, so this choice drops the controlling constraint that the configuration belongs to a cation. (Choice C) Period 5, Group 12 arises from returning only two electrons (assuming a \(2+\) charge instead of \(3+\)), which fills \(5s^2\) to give \([\text{Kr}]4d^{10}5s^2\), the configuration of cadmium (\(Z = 48\)) in Group 12; using the wrong charge magnitude stops the rebuild one electron short of the \(5p^1\) that defines the true valence shell. (Choice D) Period 5, Group 7 comes from reversing the direction of the charge and treating the \(3+\) ion as having GAINED three electrons, so that the neutral atom would have three fewer electrons than the ion; subtracting three from the ion's 46 electrons gives 43, the atomic number of technetium in Group 7, but a cation has fewer electrons than its neutral atom, so the neutral parent must have more electrons, not fewer. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must identify that the given data describe a cation, reverse the ionization by adding the correct number of electrons into the correct subshells via the aufbau order, and only then apply the period and group rules to locate the neutral element.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '3a0f7eef-704a-4722-91bd-88c874f3a554';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3a0f7eef-704a-4722-91bd-88c874f3a554';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3a0f7eef-704a-4722-91bd-88c874f3a554', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Reads the cation configuration as if it were the neutral atom, dropping the +3 charge constraint and landing on Pd (Period 5, Group 10)$a$),
  ('3a0f7eef-704a-4722-91bd-88c874f3a554', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Uses the wrong charge magnitude (assumes +2 instead of +3), returning only two electrons to fill 5s2 and stopping at Cd (Period 5, Group 12)$a$),
  ('3a0f7eef-704a-4722-91bd-88c874f3a554', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Reverses the charge sign, treating the +3 cation as having gained three electrons and subtracting from the ion's electron count to reach Tc (Period 5, Group 7)$a$);

-- 6. [Periodic Table Structure & Classification · Skill 2 · ans D] id 44fe73c7-055d-4713-8ea1-00c81338cbd4
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A neutral ground-state atom gains electrons to form a monatomic anion that is isoelectronic with argon, \(\text{Ar}\) (18 electrons). Among the period-3 elements that can form such an anion, exactly one has a neutral atom whose ground-state configuration contains precisely two unpaired electrons. A student must shade that neutral atom's single cell on a blank table showing only the s-block and p-block columns. Into which cell should it be shaded?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"Period 4, Group 16, p-block"},{"label":"B","text":"Period 3, Group 15, p-block"},{"label":"C","text":"Period 3, Group 17, p-block"},{"label":"D","text":"Period 3, Group 16, p-block"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is D because the placement requires chaining three steps. First, an anion isoelectronic with argon must reach 18 electrons, so the candidate neutral atoms are those period-3 elements lying just short of 18: chlorine (\(Z=17\), \([\text{Ne}]3s^2 3p^5\), gains one electron), sulfur (\(Z=16\), \([\text{Ne}]3s^2 3p^4\), gains two), and phosphorus (\(Z=15\), \([\text{Ne}]3s^2 3p^3\), gains three). Second, the controlling constraint selects among them by Hund's rule applied to the three \(3p\) orbitals of the neutral atom: \(3p^3\) has three unpaired electrons, \(3p^4\) has two unpaired (one orbital doubly occupied, two singly), and \(3p^5\) has one unpaired. Only \(3p^4\) gives exactly two unpaired electrons, identifying the neutral atom as sulfur. Third, sulfur is placed from its own configuration: the outermost occupied shell is \(n=3\), fixing Period 3; the \(3s^2 3p^4\) valence shell (six valence electrons) places it in Group 16; and because the differentiating electron enters a \(p\) subshell it lies in the p-block. (Choice A) Period 4 results from treating the argon to which the anion is isoelectronic as a noble-gas core and assuming the valence electrons must occupy the next shell, \(n=4\); sulfur's outermost electrons remain in \(n=3\), so the period is 3, not 4. (Choice B) Group 15 corresponds to phosphorus, \(3s^2 3p^3\); this selection comes from choosing the element that gains the most electrons to reach argon (the \(3-\) anion) rather than the one whose neutral atom has exactly two unpaired electrons, confusing anion charge magnitude with the unpaired-electron criterion. (Choice C) Group 17 corresponds to chlorine, \(3s^2 3p^5\), which has only one unpaired electron; selecting it requires miscounting the \(3p\) occupancy under Hund's rule and attributing two unpaired electrons to a \(p^5\) configuration. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate isoelectronic reasoning, a Hund's-rule unpaired-electron count, and block/group/period assignment, carrying the intermediate result of each step into the next to reach the correct cell.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '44fe73c7-055d-4713-8ea1-00c81338cbd4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '44fe73c7-055d-4713-8ea1-00c81338cbd4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('44fe73c7-055d-4713-8ea1-00c81338cbd4', 'A', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Assigns the period from the noble gas the anion is isoelectronic with, treating Ar as a core and putting valence electrons in n=4 instead of reading the neutral atom's own outermost shell (n=3).$a$),
  ('44fe73c7-055d-4713-8ea1-00c81338cbd4', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Applies the wrong discriminator by selecting the element that gains the most electrons to reach Ar (the 3- anion, phosphorus) instead of the one whose neutral atom has exactly two unpaired electrons.$a$),
  ('44fe73c7-055d-4713-8ea1-00c81338cbd4', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Miscounts unpaired electrons under Hund's rule, wrongly attributing two unpaired electrons to a 3p^5 (chlorine) configuration that actually has only one.$a$);

-- 7. [Periodic Table Structure & Classification · Skill 2 · ans C] id 70173c87-6ae6-4920-8c2a-681ea1d1eefa
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$In period 6 the subshells are filled in the aufbau order \(6s \rightarrow 4f \rightarrow 5d \rightarrow 6p\), beginning with cesium (\(Z=55\)). A chemist scanning across this period wants the atomic number of the first element whose differentiating electron enters the \(6p\) subshell, marking the left edge of the period-6 p-block. Reading the filling order correctly, which atomic number most likely identifies that first p-block element?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(Z=57\\), reached immediately after the \\(6s\\) (2) subshell is complete, since the d-block opens each transition row."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(Z=71\\), reached after the \\(6s\\) (2) and \\(4f\\) (14) subshells are complete but before any \\(5d\\) electrons are added."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(Z=81\\), reached after the \\(6s\\) (2), \\(4f\\) (14), and \\(5d\\) (10) subshells are all complete."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(Z=87\\), reached after the \\(6s\\) (2), \\(4f\\) (14), \\(5d\\) (10), and \\(6p\\) (6) subshells are all complete."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The block structure of the periodic table mirrors the order in which subshells fill, and the p-block element begins only when the differentiating electron first enters the \(np\) subshell. The answer to this question is C because the period-6 aufbau order is \(6s \rightarrow 4f \rightarrow 5d \rightarrow 6p\), and the p-block opens only after every subshell that fills ahead of \(6p\) is complete. Starting from cesium (\(Z=55\)), the \(6s\) subshell takes 2 electrons (\(Z=55\rightarrow 56\)), the \(4f\) subshell takes 14 (\(Z=57\rightarrow 70\)), and the \(5d\) subshell takes 10 (\(Z=71\rightarrow 80\)). The next added electron, at \(Z=55+2+14+10=81\), is therefore the first to enter \(6p\), making \(Z=81\) (thallium) the left edge of the period-6 p-block. (Choice A) \(Z=57\) is reached right after the \(6s\) subshell fills, but in period 6 the \(4f\) subshell (14 electrons) and then the \(5d\) subshell (10 electrons) both fill before \(6p\); \(Z=57\) opens the f-block, so this choice ignores both the inserted \(4f\) and the \(5d\) subshells. (Choice B) \(Z=71\) is reached after the \(6s\) and \(4f\) subshells fill, but \(5d\) (which holds 10 electrons across \(Z=71\rightarrow 80\)) still fills before \(6p\); \(Z=71\) opens the d-block, not the p-block, so this choice drops the entire \(5d\) capacity. (Choice D) \(Z=87\) lies one past the end of period 6: completing \(6p\) (6 electrons across \(Z=81\rightarrow 86\)) finishes the row at radon (\(Z=86\)), so \(Z=87\) is francium, the first element of period 7, not the first p-block element of period 6. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the period-6 filling order with the capacity of each inserted subshell to count to the exact atomic number where the p-block begins.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '70173c87-6ae6-4920-8c2a-681ea1d1eefa';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '70173c87-6ae6-4920-8c2a-681ea1d1eefa';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('70173c87-6ae6-4920-8c2a-681ea1d1eefa', 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Counts only the 6s subshell before the differentiating electron, ignoring both the inserted 4f and the 5d subshells, and mislabels the f-block opener as the p-block opener.$a$),
  ('70173c87-6ae6-4920-8c2a-681ea1d1eefa', 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Drops the 5d subshell capacity, stopping the count at the start of the d-block (after 6s and 4f) instead of carrying through 5d to the start of the p-block.$a$),
  ('70173c87-6ae6-4920-8c2a-681ea1d1eefa', 'D', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Adds the full 6p capacity as well, counting to the element after the period ends, so it lands one atomic number past radon at the first element of period 7.$a$);

-- 8. [Periodic Table Structure & Classification · Skill 2 · ans B] id f881d1fa-8c66-4e0d-b621-d485539a6d53
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$Suppose a chemistry instructor poses a "what-if" exercise: keep every rule of the aufbau filling order exactly as it is, but imagine that each f-type orbital set held only 5 orbitals instead of 7. In this hypothetical, period 6 would still begin at an alkali metal and end at a noble gas, filling the same lineup of subshells in the same energetic sequence. Under this single altered rule, period 6 would most likely contain how many elements?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"32 elements"},{"label":"B","text":"28 elements"},{"label":"C","text":"20 elements"},{"label":"D","text":"18 elements"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is B because the length of any period equals the summed electron capacity of every subshell that fills across it, so the problem must be solved by first identifying the correct filling sequence for period 6 and only then applying the altered f-orbital constraint. Period 6 is the first period whose aufbau sequence inserts both an f-block and a d-block, filling \(6s\), then \(4f\), then \(5d\), and finally \(6p\). Each orbital holds 2 electrons, so capacity is twice the orbital count: \(6s\) (1 orbital) gives 2, \(5d\) (5 orbitals) gives 10, and \(6p\) (3 orbitals) gives 6. The single hypothetical change reduces the f-orbital set from 7 orbitals to 5 orbitals, so the \(4f\) capacity becomes \(2 \times 5 = 10\) rather than the real 14. Chaining these: \(2 + 10 + 10 + 6 = 28\). (Choice A) 32 is the real, unaltered length of period 6, obtained by using the actual \(4f\) capacity of 14 \((2 + 14 + 10 + 6 = 32)\); this drops the controlling hypothetical constraint that each f set now holds only 5 orbitals. (Choice C) 20 sums only the two inner subshells that fill in period 6, the altered \(4f\) (10) and the \(5d\) (10) \((10 + 10 = 20)\), but omits the \(6s\) and \(6p\) subshells that also fill across the period; period length must include every subshell that fills, not just the inner-block ones. (Choice D) 18 applies the altered \(4f\) capacity (10) and keeps \(6s\) and \(6p\) \((2 + 10 + 6 = 18)\) but discards the \(5d\) block, as if period 6 followed the period-4/period-5 pattern of one inner block between the s- and p-blocks; period 6 is distinctive precisely because it inserts both an f-block and a d-block, so the \(5d\) capacity of 10 cannot be dropped. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the aufbau filling order, the orbital-count-to-capacity rule, and a modified constraint, then carry intermediate subshell capacities into a final summation.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'f881d1fa-8c66-4e0d-b621-d485539a6d53';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f881d1fa-8c66-4e0d-b621-d485539a6d53';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f881d1fa-8c66-4e0d-b621-d485539a6d53', 'A', 'misconception', $a$dropped-the-altered-constraint-and-used-the-real-f-capacity-14-yielding-the-actual-period-6-length-of-32$a$),
  ('f881d1fa-8c66-4e0d-b621-d485539a6d53', 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a$summed-only-the-inner-f-and-d-blocks-altered-4f-10-plus-5d-10-omitting-the-6s-and-6p-subshells-that-also-fill-across-the-period$a$),
  ('f881d1fa-8c66-4e0d-b621-d485539a6d53', 'D', 'partial_truth', $a$applied-the-altered-f-but-dropped-the-5d-block-treating-period-6-like-the-single-inner-block-pattern-of-periods-4-and-5$a$);

-- 9. [Periodic Trends · Skill 4 · ans A] id 62afd426-b0e7-4e1e-aef0-df8c7c9bdab8
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist classifies four bonds using the Hannay-Smyth relation, where the percent ionic character of a bond is \(\text{16}(\Delta\text{EN}) + \text{3.5}(\Delta\text{EN})^2\) and a bond is taken to be predominantly covalent only when this value stays below \(\text{50}\%\). The Pauling electronegativities are \(\text{Be} = \text{1.57}\), \(\text{B} = \text{2.04}\), \(\text{Li} = \text{0.98}\), \(\text{O} = \text{3.44}\), \(\text{F} = \text{3.98}\), and \(\text{Cl} = \text{3.16}\). Of the bonds below, which one is most likely the most polar bond that is still predominantly covalent?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The bond in \\(\\text{BF}_3\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"The bond in \\(\\text{BeO}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"The bond in \\(\\text{LiCl}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"The bond in \\(\\text{BeF}_2\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Variations of Chemical Properties with Group and Row'. Bond character tracks the electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) between the bonded atoms, and the Hannay-Smyth relation converts that difference into a percent ionic character, \(\text{16}(\Delta\text{EN}) + \text{3.5}(\Delta\text{EN})^2\); the controlling constraint is that a bond counts as predominantly covalent only when this percentage stays below \(\text{50}\%\), so the target bond is the one with the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) that does not cross that line. The answer to this question is A because the four \(\Delta\text{EN}\) values must first be computed from the supplied electronegativities, then each fed through the formula, and finally screened against the \(\text{50}\%\) cutoff before ranking the survivors. For the \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond, \(\Delta\text{EN} = \text{3.98} - \text{2.04} = \text{1.94}\), giving \(\text{16}(\text{1.94}) + \text{3.5}(\text{1.94})^2 = \text{31.0} + \text{13.2} = \text{44.2}\%\); this stays below \(\text{50}\%\), so the bond is predominantly covalent, and at \(\Delta\text{EN} = \text{1.94}\) it is more polar than the only other sub-\(\text{50}\%\) candidate, making it the most polar predominantly-covalent bond. (Choice B) The \(\text{Be}-\text{O}\) bond has \(\Delta\text{EN} = \text{3.44} - \text{1.57} = \text{1.87}\), yielding \(\text{16}(\text{1.87}) + \text{3.5}(\text{1.87})^2 = \text{29.9} + \text{12.2} = \text{42.2}\%\); this bond is also predominantly covalent, but its smaller \(\Delta\text{EN}\) makes it slightly less polar than the \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) bond, so it is the runner-up rather than the most polar of the covalent pair. (Choice C) The \(\text{Li}-\text{Cl}\) bond has \(\Delta\text{EN} = \text{3.16} - \text{0.98} = \text{2.18}\), giving \(\text{16}(\text{2.18}) + \text{3.5}(\text{2.18})^2 = \text{34.9} + \text{16.6} = \text{51.5}\%\); because this just exceeds \(\text{50}\%\), the bond is classified as predominantly ionic and is excluded by the covalent constraint despite its substantial polarity. (Choice D) The \(\text{Be}-\text{F}\) bond has the largest electronegativity difference, \(\Delta\text{EN} = \text{3.98} - \text{1.57} = \text{2.41}\), producing \(\text{16}(\text{2.41}) + \text{3.5}(\text{2.41})^2 = \text{38.6} + \text{20.3} = \text{58.9}\%\); selecting it reflects the instinct that the bond with the greatest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) must be the answer, but that value places the bond well into the ionic region, so the covalent constraint rules it out. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must carry each electronegativity difference through a two-term quadratic relation, apply a threshold to filter the results, and only then rank the qualifying bonds to identify the one the question specifies.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = '62afd426-b0e7-4e1e-aef0-df8c7c9bdab8';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '62afd426-b0e7-4e1e-aef0-df8c7c9bdab8';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('62afd426-b0e7-4e1e-aef0-df8c7c9bdab8', 'B', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly identifies a predominantly-covalent bond but stops before comparing polarity magnitudes, choosing the lower-ΔEN covalent bond instead of the most polar one$a$),
  ('62afd426-b0e7-4e1e-aef0-df8c7c9bdab8', 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a$Computes a value just above the 50% cutoff (51.5%) and treats it as still covalent, dropping the threshold direction by one classification band$a$),
  ('62afd426-b0e7-4e1e-aef0-df8c7c9bdab8', 'D', 'misconception', $a$Applies the naive biggest-ΔEN-is-most-ionic-or-polar heuristic and ignores the controlling 50% covalent constraint, selecting the bond that is actually predominantly ionic$a$);

-- 10. [Periodic Trends · Skill 2 · ans A] id a24ff0b8-0990-4256-9a12-7eb48b5d512d
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A discharge cell strips one electron from a gaseous sodium atom and delivers that electron to a single gas-phase atom of one period-3 element, forming an isolated \(\text{Na}^+ / \text{X}^-\) pair. For sodium, \(\text{IE}_1 = +496\ \text{kJ/mol}\). The electron affinities of the candidate acceptors are \(\text{Si} = -134\), \(\text{P} = -72\), \(\text{S} = -200\), and \(\text{Cl} = -349\ \text{kJ/mol}\) (negative values denote energy released). Treating the net electronic energy of the transfer as \(\text{IE}_1(\text{Na}) + \text{EA}(\text{X})\), and choosing the acceptor for which the half-filled-subshell effect makes the overall transfer release the least energy, the net energy for that pairing is most likely$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+424\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\), for capture by phosphorus"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+296\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\), for capture by sulfur"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+362\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\), for capture by silicon"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-72\\ \\text{kJ/mol}\\), for capture by phosphorus"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Variations of Chemical Properties with Group and Row'. The answer to this question is A because the problem must be chained in three steps. First, identify which acceptor releases the least energy on electron capture: although electron affinity generally grows more negative across period 3, phosphorus \(\left([\text{Ne}]3s^2 3p^3\right)\) interrupts the trend. Its three \(3p\) electrons singly occupy the three \(3p\) orbitals, so an incoming electron has no empty \(3p\) orbital and must pair within an already-occupied one; the resulting electron-electron repulsion cancels much of the stabilization nuclear attraction would otherwise provide, leaving \(\text{P}\) with the least exothermic electron affinity of the four (\(-72\ \text{kJ/mol}\), far less negative than its neighbors \(\text{Si}\) at \(-134\) and \(\text{S}\) at \(-200\)). Second, recognize that the discharge cell first pays the sodium ionization cost, \(+496\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Third, sum the two steps for the phosphorus pairing: \(+496 + (-72) = +424\ \text{kJ/mol}\), the largest (least exothermic) net value among the choices, which is exactly what "releases the least energy" demands. (Choice B) Sulfur has the most negative electron affinity of the realistic acceptors (\(-200\ \text{kJ/mol}\)), giving the most exothermic net transfer, \(+496 + (-200) = +296\ \text{kJ/mol}\); selecting it reverses the requirement, because the most-favorable acceptor releases the most energy, not the least. (Choice C) Silicon \(\left(3p^2\right)\) still has an empty \(3p\) orbital for the incoming electron, so it carries no half-filled pairing penalty; its electron affinity (\(-134\ \text{kJ/mol}\)) gives \(+496 + (-134) = +362\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Placing the anomaly at the \(np^2\) group-14 atom rather than the \(np^3\) group-15 atom misidentifies which configuration resists an added electron. (Choice D) The value \(-72\ \text{kJ/mol}\) is phosphorus's electron affinity reported alone; it omits the required ionization step for sodium and therefore stops at the intermediate quantity rather than summing both contributions of the transfer. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the half-filled-subshell electron-affinity anomaly with an ionization-energy term and carry an intermediate value through a multi-step net-energy calculation to reach the answer.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'a24ff0b8-0990-4256-9a12-7eb48b5d512d';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'a24ff0b8-0990-4256-9a12-7eb48b5d512d';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('a24ff0b8-0990-4256-9a12-7eb48b5d512d', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Selects the most-negative-EA acceptor (sulfur) and reverses 'least energy released,' computing 496 + (-200) = +296$a$),
  ('a24ff0b8-0990-4256-9a12-7eb48b5d512d', 'C', 'misconception', $a$Mislocates the half-filled anomaly at the np^2 (group 14, silicon) atom instead of the np^3 (group 15) atom, computing 496 + (-134) = +362$a$),
  ('a24ff0b8-0990-4256-9a12-7eb48b5d512d', 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a$Correctly identifies phosphorus but reports its EA alone, dropping the sodium ionization-energy step from the net-energy sum$a$);

-- 11. [Periodic Trends · Skill 4 · ans B] id c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$A chemist must select, from four binary bonds, the one that lies furthest toward the ionic side of the bonding continuum while still classifying as polar covalent under the customary rule that an electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}\) above about \(1.7\) is ionic and a \(\Delta\text{EN}\) below about \(0.4\) is nonpolar covalent. The relevant Pauling electronegativities are \(\text{Al}=1.61\), \(\text{Cl}=3.16\), \(\text{Be}=1.57\), \(\text{F}=3.98\), \(\text{C}=2.55\), \(\text{O}=3.44\), \(\text{H}=2.20\), and \(\text{S}=2.58\). Which bond should the chemist choose as the most polar covalent?$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(\\text{Be}-\\text{F}\\) bond, with \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}=2.41\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"The \\(\\text{Al}-\\text{Cl}\\) bond, with \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}=1.55\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"The \\(\\text{C}-\\text{O}\\) bond, with \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}=0.89\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"The \\(\\text{H}-\\text{S}\\) bond, with \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}=0.38\\)"}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Variations of Chemical Properties with Group and Row'. The answer to this question is B because the bond character must be filtered through a two-sided window before any bond can be ranked: a bond counts as polar covalent only when its \(\Delta\text{EN}\) sits above the nonpolar ceiling (about \(0.4\)) and below the ionic floor (about \(1.7\)), and among the bonds that survive that window the most polar covalent one is the bond with the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\). Computing each difference gives \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}: 3.16-1.61=1.55\); \(\text{Be}-\text{F}: 3.98-1.57=2.41\); \(\text{C}-\text{O}: 3.44-2.55=0.89\); and \(\text{H}-\text{S}: 2.58-2.20=0.38\). The \(\text{Be}-\text{F}\) value of \(2.41\) exceeds the \(\sim 1.7\) ionic floor, so that bond is classified ionic and is disqualified as a polar covalent candidate despite having the numerically largest difference. The \(\text{H}-\text{S}\) value of \(0.38\) falls below the \(\sim 0.4\) nonpolar ceiling, so that bond is essentially nonpolar covalent and is also outside the window. That leaves \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\) at \(1.55\) and \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) at \(0.89\) as the only genuine polar covalent bonds, and \(1.55 > 0.89\), so the \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\) bond carries the greater unequal sharing while remaining short of full electron transfer, making it the most polar covalent of the four. (Choice A) The \(\text{Be}-\text{F}\) difference of \(2.41\) is the largest of the set, but it lies above the \(\sim 1.7\) cutoff, which places the bond in the ionic regime rather than the polar covalent regime; selecting it treats the largest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) as automatically the most polar covalent and ignores that crossing the upper bound removes a bond from the polar covalent category entirely. (Choice C) The \(\text{C}-\text{O}\) difference of \(0.89\) does qualify as polar covalent, but at \(0.89\) it is well below the \(1.55\) of \(\text{Al}-\text{Cl}\), so its shared electrons are distributed more evenly; it is polar covalent yet not the most polar covalent. (Choice D) The \(\text{H}-\text{S}\) difference of \(0.38\) lies below the \(\sim 0.4\) nonpolar ceiling, so the electrons are shared nearly evenly and the bond is best described as nonpolar covalent; equating the smallest \(\Delta\text{EN}\) with the most polar bond reverses the relationship between electronegativity difference and bond polarity. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must compute and compare several electronegativity differences and then apply an upper and a lower threshold together to identify which value governs the classification.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 4,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
WHERE id = 'c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e', 'A', 'misconception', $a$Largest electronegativity difference is automatically the most polar covalent, ignoring that exceeding the ~1.7 ceiling reclassifies the bond as ionic$a$),
  ('c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e', 'C', 'partial_truth', $a$Correctly excludes the ionic bond but mis-ranks among the surviving polar covalent bonds, picking the smaller qualifying difference$a$),
  ('c4c15193-c563-4efd-bbdc-03747dbbe21e', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a$Treats the smallest electronegativity difference as the most polar, reversing the link between delta-EN and bond polarity$a$);

-- 12. [Types of Elements · Skill 2 · ans D] id 24c0e846-c9e3-4a32-8569-9dc979ac998a
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = $qt$An element with the ground-state valence configuration \(3s^2 3p^4\) forms a highest oxide of formula \(\text{XO}_3\). A \(0.100\ \text{g}\) sample of this oxide \((\text{molar mass} = 80.07\ \text{g/mol})\) is dissolved in water and the solution is diluted to \(0.250\ \text{L}\). Assuming the oxide reacts characteristically for its elemental classification and that any acid formed ionizes completely, the resulting solution will most likely register a pH closest to$qt$,
  options = $op$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(7.0\\), because the oxide is amphoteric and neutralizes itself in water."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(2.3\\), because the oxide is acidic and forms a strong monoprotic oxyacid."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(12.0\\), because the oxide is basic and releases hydroxide ions into solution."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(2.0\\), because the oxide is acidic and forms a strong diprotic oxyacid."}]$op$::jsonb,
  explanation = $ex$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. The answer to this question is D because three facts must be chained. First, the valence configuration \(3s^2 3p^4\) places the element in period 3, group 16 (sulfur), which lies on the nonmetal side of the periodic table; classification of an element as a nonmetal predicts that its oxide is an acidic oxide (an acid anhydride). Second, the highest oxide \(\text{XO}_3\) is therefore \(\text{SO}_3\), the anhydride of sulfuric acid: \(\text{SO}_3 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\), and \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\) is a strong DIPROTIC acid. Third, the quantitative chain: \(\text{mol} = 0.100\ \text{g} \div 80.07\ \text{g/mol} = 1.25 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{mol}\), giving \([\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4] = (1.25 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{mol}) \div 0.250\ \text{L} = 5.00 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{M}\). Because the acid is diprotic and ionizes completely, \([\text{H}^+] = 2 \times (5.00 \times 10^{-3}) = 1.00 \times 10^{-2}\ \text{M}\), so \(\text{pH} = -\log(1.00 \times 10^{-2}) = 2.0\). (Choice A) This value treats a clear-cut nonmetal oxide as amphoteric or self-neutralizing; although borderline metalloid oxides such as \(\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3\) are amphoteric, \(\text{SO}_3\) is an unambiguous acidic oxide and yields an acidic, not neutral, solution. (Choice B) This value drops the diprotic constraint, treating the oxyacid as monoprotic so that \([\text{H}^+] = 5.00 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{M}\) and \(\text{pH} = 2.3\); however, \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\) supplies two protons per formula unit, so the proton concentration is twice as large and the pH correspondingly lower. (Choice C) This value reverses the metal/nonmetal classification, treating the group 16 element as a metal whose oxide is basic; a basic oxide would generate \([\text{OH}^-] = 1.00 \times 10^{-2}\ \text{M}\), \(\text{pOH} = 2.0\), and \(\text{pH} = 12.0\), but a nonmetal oxide produces an oxyacid and \(\text{H}^+\), not hydroxide. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must classify the element from its electron configuration, predict the acid-base character and proticity of its oxide, and then carry a multi-step mass-to-moles-to-concentration-to-pH calculation in which each intermediate result feeds the next.$ex$,
  cognitive_skill = 2,
  difficulty = 'hard',
  estimated_time_seconds = 160
